Build a Hive Desktop applet.
{{template "app" .}}
An applet is a web page that opens over the app, from the command palette or a key
the user binds. It runs on the user's own machine with their full access, and that
is intended: do what the request needs.

## Files

Write the applet to {{.Env.AppletsDir}}/<id>/. The directory name is the applet's id:
a slug of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens. It holds an `applet.yaml` and the
page. The app watches these files, so the applet appears as soon as the file is
valid. Nothing runs until the user opens it or puts it in the title bar.

```yaml
version: 1
title: Containers
icon: container
width: 1200
height: 760
```

Unknown keys are errors.

- `version`: must be `1`.
- `title`: the name in the palette.
- `icon`: optional glyph: `terminal`, `git-branch`, `git-compare`, `folder`, `file-text`, `search`, `database`, `gauge`, `activity`, `flask-conical`, `hammer`, `container`, `cloud`, `bug`, `zap`, `package`, `globe`, `external-link`, `git-pull-request`, or `play`.
- `cwd`: where commands run. Omit it when the applet is about the code in front of the user: commands then run in the on-screen session's checkout, and in the applet's directory when no session is on screen. Set `"~"` or an absolute path when the applet works anywhere. A relative path is relative to the applet's directory.
- `entry`: the page, default `index.html`.
- `width` and `height`: the panel in pixels. The default fills the window.
- `titlebar`: offers a page for the title bar, with `entry` (default: the applet's own `entry`) and `width` in pixels (default 28, one icon button). See "In the title bar".
- `commands`: palette rows the user can bind to a key, each with `id` (a slug), `title`, and optional `icon` and `keywords`. See "In the command palette".

## The page

It is a normal web page, served from the applet's directory: relative scripts, ES
modules, stylesheets, images, audio and network requests all work. There is no build
step, so either write plain files or commit the built output. Editing a page, script
or stylesheet reloads it on screen and stops the processes it started. Hive draws a
close button over the top right corner of the page, so leave about 52px clear there.

`window.hive` exists before your scripts run. Every call returns a promise.

- `hive.exec(command, {cwd, env, stdin, timeout})` runs a shell command and resolves to `{stdout, stderr, code}`. A non-zero `code` does not reject. `timeout` is in milliseconds and defaults to 5 minutes.
- `hive.spawn(command, {cwd, env, onStdout, onStderr, onExit})` starts a long-running process and resolves to `{write(text), kill()}`. Output arrives as text in arbitrary chunks. Closing the applet stops it.
- `hive.readFile(path)` and `hive.writeFile(path, text)` handle text files. A relative path is relative to the applet's directory, and `~` and absolute paths work.
- `hive.terminal(command, {cwd})` opens the command in the pop-up terminal, for anything interactive.
- `hive.toast(message, {severity, body})` shows a Hive toast. `severity` is `info`, `success`, `warning`, or `error`.
- `hive.copy(text)` puts text on the clipboard.
- `hive.context()` resolves to `{id, title, dir, cwd, sessionSlug}`.
- `hive.close()` closes the applet. In a title bar page it closes the page's dropdown.
- `hive.surface` is `page`, `titlebar` or `dropdown`, for one file that serves several.
- `hive.open()` opens the applet's panel from any of them.
- `hive.broadcast(data)` sends to the applet's other open frames, which listen with `hive.on('message', fn)`.
- `hive.on('session', fn)` reports the slug of the session on screen when it changes. `hive.on` returns a function that stops listening.
- `hive.commands()` resolves to Hive's commands as `{id, title, group, keys}`, and `hive.command(id)` runs one and rejects an id that does not exist, so a page can do what the command palette can.
- `hive.openPalette(query)` opens the command palette on a query. A first character of `+`, `@` or `>` picks its tab.
- `hive.setCommands(rows)` and `hive.on('command', fn)` add to the palette. See "In the command palette".
- `hive.navigate(path)` goes to a route of the app: `/hub`, `/terminal/<session-slug>`, `/settings/<section>`.

Commands run through `sh -c` in the directory `cwd` describes, with `$HIVE_APPLET_DIR`
set. Pass values to a command through `env` and quote them in the command
(`hive.exec('docker restart "$ID"', {env: {ID: id}})`), because a value built into the
command text is shell injection by accident.

`localStorage` works and persists per applet. Cookies and IndexedDB do not, because
the frame has an opaque origin. Read and write files for anything larger. A page can
navigate to another HTML file of its own, but cannot `fetch` one. If the frame
navigates to any other site, that page gets no `window.hive`.

Hive sets its theme on the page's root element as CSS variables, and updates them when
the user changes theme. They are empty for a moment while the page loads, so give each
`var()` a fallback: `--hv-app`, `--hv-sidebar`, `--hv-pane`, `--hv-raised`, `--hv-list`, `--hv-chip`, `--hv-hover`, `--hv-row`, `--hv-row-hover`, `--hv-card`, `--hv-border`, `--hv-strong`, `--hv-text`, `--hv-text-2`, `--hv-text-3`, `--hv-text-4`, `--hv-accent`, `--hv-accent-contrast`, `--hv-accent-tint`, `--hv-selection`, `--hv-backdrop`, `--hv-code-key`, `--hv-code-string`, `--hv-code-comment`, and `--hv-severity-info`, `-success`, `-warning`, `-error` and `-running`, each with a `-tint`.

Escape closes the applet unless your keydown handler calls `preventDefault()`. Use
that to let Escape back out of a dialog or a filter first. Command, Control and Option
chords reach Hive's shortcuts unless your handler calls `preventDefault()`, and while a
panel is open that only runs the palette, the key that closes the panel, and this applet's
commands. So call `preventDefault()` on any chord the page uses for itself, such as Command-K
for its own search. Other keys stay in the page. Audio needs a user gesture first, as on any
page.

## In the title bar

Use this for something that is always there: an indicator, media controls, a shortcut to
another part of the app. With `titlebar:` in `applet.yaml`, the applet can show a page in
Hive's title bar once the user turns it on in Settings > Applets, or adds its id to
`applets.titlebar` in `settings.yaml` when they ask you to. It then loads with the app and
keeps running until they turn it off, so keep what it polls cheap.

- The slot is 28px tall. Set `html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; background: transparent; }` so the page sits on the bar, and match Hive's 28px controls with a 8px radius and a `--hv-chip` hover fill. Use `--hv-*` colours.
- `hive.resize({width})` changes the slot's width. Width `0` hides the slot and keeps the page running, for an indicator that only shows when there is something to say.
- `hive.dropdown({entry, width, height})` opens a panel under the bar and a second call closes it, so call it from a click. It closes when the user clicks elsewhere or presses Escape. `hive.resize({width, height})` from its page resizes it. It loads fresh on each open, so anything that must keep going, such as audio or a long-running process, belongs in the title bar page. Keep the two in step with `hive.broadcast`.
- `localStorage` is shared between an applet's frames, and the `storage` event fires as it does in a browser.
- Closing a panel or dropdown stops only what that frame spawned.

If the applet is only for the title bar, the palette still opens its `entry` as a panel, so
make that page useful too: settings or a larger view.

## In the command palette

Use `commands:` in `applet.yaml` for what a user would want to do from anywhere, or bind to a key. The row is always there, and the user binds it as `applet.<id>.<command>`. Do not suggest a chord or write one to `settings.yaml` unless they ask. The palette can show the title without the applet's name, so write "Next radio station" and not "Next".

`hive.on('command', (id) => ...)` receives it. It goes to the title bar page if that is on. Otherwise the panel opens and its page receives it, so the applet's `entry` must handle every command too, or be the same page as the title bar's. From the title bar page, a command can call `hive.dropdown()` to open the dropdown.

Use `hive.setCommands(rows)` for rows only a running page knows about, such as stations, containers or recent files. Each call replaces the rows that page set before, and they go when the page closes or reloads. They cannot be bound. A row needs `id` and `title`, and can have `group` (default: the applet's title), `scope` (`actions`, the default, or `goto` for a row that names a thing), `kind` (the small word at the right of such a row), `hint`, `keywords` and `icon`. Selecting one calls the same `hive.on('command')` with its id.

## Sharing

The user shares an applet from Settings > Applets, as one `.hiveapplet` file. An export leaves out hidden files, `.log` files and links, along with `localStorage`, and takes everything else in the directory. So keep what a page saves for this machine in `localStorage`, a hidden file or a `.log` file, never in a plain file, and never put a token or a personal path in a file that would ship. Prefer `~` or the session's checkout to a path from this machine in `cwd`. Import does not keep the executable bit, so run a script with `sh script.sh`. Refer the user to Import in Settings when they want an applet someone sent them, and do not unpack one into the applets directory yourself: Import shows them what they are about to trust.

## Check your work

1. Run the commands the page uses and confirm their output is what the page parses.
2. If the `list_applets` tool of the app's MCP server is available, call it and fix any `error` it reports. Without it, the palette row for a broken applet opens and shows the same error.
3. Tell the user the applet's name, and that they open it from the command palette and can bind it to a key as `applet.<id>`, and name any `commands` it has. For a title bar page, also tell them to turn it on in Settings > Applets.
